{"qid":"Q6737189","names":["Maitraka Dynasty"],"title":"Maitraka Dynasty","endonym":null,"capsule":"The Maitraka dynasty ruled Saurashtra and Gujarat in western India from their capital at Vallabhi from around 475 CE, founded by Bhatarka, a Gupta military governor who asserted independence as the Gupta imperial system collapsed. Vallabhi developed into a major centre of Hinayana Buddhist learning — Chinese pilgrim Xuanzang, who visited in the 640s, praised Shiladitya I as an exemplary ruler — while the dynasty itself was predominantly Shaiva and patronized Brahmanical scholarship alongside Buddhist institutions. The university at Vallabhi rivalled Nalanda and attracted students from across Asia. The dynasty was weakened by repeated Arab naval raids on the Gujarat coast from the early eighth century and collapsed around 776 CE; no source records its final end explicitly, but Shiladitya VI is the last attested ruler.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The Maitraka core territory in Saurashtra (Kathiawar peninsula) is well-attested through copper-plate grants and Chinese pilgrimage records, but the dynasty's extent into mainland Gujarat and Rajasthan is less precisely documented.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Maitraka dynasty","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maitraka_dynasty"},{"label":"Britannica: Maitraka dynasty","url":"https://www.britannica.com/topic/Maitraka-dynasty"},{"label":"Britannica: Vallabhi","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Vallabhi"}],"britannica":"topic/Maitraka-dynasty","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Maitraka dynasty","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Bhatarka","nativeN